Reference-typed struct fields
=============================

When a struct has a `&NrfInner` field, the field itself is a
reference. Assigning a new value to that field must:

  - decrement the old ref's buffer (potentially freeing it),
  - increment the new ref's buffer (unless ownership is being
    transferred from a fresh `new`).

Construction of a struct that contains a `&NrfInner` field also
participates: the constructor argument transfers ownership
in (if fresh) or borrows (if it's an existing ref).

These tests pin the contract through the *visible* behaviour
of field reads after a sequence of mutations. If the field's
refcount handling is wrong, reads land on freed memory.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct NrfInner
        v: int

    struct NrfOuter
        child: &NrfInner

Construction with a fresh inner ref
-----------------------------------

    #test
    test_construct_with_fresh_inner() -> unit
        val o: NrfOuter = NrfOuter(new NrfInner(42))
        assert(o.child.v == 42, s"o.child.v should be 42, got ${o.child.v}")

Construction with a borrowed inner ref
--------------------------------------

The inner is referenced from two places: the local `i` and
the outer struct's `child` field. Refcount needs to reflect
two owners.

    #test
    test_construct_with_borrowed_inner() -> unit
        val i: &NrfInner = new NrfInner(7)
        val o: NrfOuter = NrfOuter(i)
        // Both bindings see the same buffer
        assert(o.child.v == 7, s"o.child.v should be 7, got ${o.child.v}")
        assert(i.v == 7, s"i.v should be 7, got ${i.v}")
        // Mutation visible through both
        o.child.v = 99
        assert(i.v == 99, s"i.v should be 99 after o.child mutation, got ${i.v}")

Field reassignment to a fresh ref
---------------------------------

    #test
    test_field_reassign_to_fresh() -> unit
        var o: NrfOuter = NrfOuter(new NrfInner(1))
        o.child = new NrfInner(2)
        o.child = new NrfInner(3)
        assert(o.child.v == 3, s"o.child.v should be 3 after reassignments, got ${o.child.v}")

Field reassignment to a borrowed ref
------------------------------------

After `o.child = other`, both `o.child` and `other` should
share the buffer. The old `o.child` buffer should be freed
(refcount drops). The new buffer's refcount should now
reflect two owners.

    #test
    test_field_reassign_to_borrowed() -> unit
        var o: NrfOuter = NrfOuter(new NrfInner(10))
        val other: &NrfInner = new NrfInner(20)
        o.child = other
        assert(o.child.v == 20, s"o.child.v should be 20 (= other.v), got ${o.child.v}")
        // Mutation visible through both
        o.child.v = 30
        assert(other.v == 30, s"other.v should be 30, got ${other.v}")

Self-assign field
-----------------

If `o.child = o.child` is decr-then-incr, the inner buffer
frees and then increments freed memory.

    #test
    test_field_self_assign() -> unit
        var o: NrfOuter = NrfOuter(new NrfInner(123))
        o.child = o.child
        assert(o.child.v == 123, s"o.child.v should still be 123 after self-assign, got ${o.child.v}")
        // After self-assign, mutation still works (buffer not freed)
        o.child.v = 456
        assert(o.child.v == 456, s"o.child.v should be 456 after mutation, got ${o.child.v}")

NrfOuter drops -> inner field is dropped
-------------------------------------

When the outer struct's binding goes out of scope (or is
reassigned to a fresh NrfOuter), the inner field's ref must
drop. We can't observe the drop directly (no leak counter at
the language level), but we *can* observe that the new outer
contains the right inner, and that nothing crashes.

    #test
    test_outer_reassign_drops_old_inner() -> unit
        var o: NrfOuter = NrfOuter(new NrfInner(1))
        o = NrfOuter(new NrfInner(2))
        o = NrfOuter(new NrfInner(3))
        // The old NrfInner(1) and NrfInner(2) should have been freed. We can
        // only check the final state.
        assert(o.child.v == 3, s"final o.child.v should be 3, got ${o.child.v}")
